  "name": "Ghost Dance songs (1889-90 movement and its survivals)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Songs of the Ghost Dance, the religious movement begun by the Northern Paiute prophet Wovoka in western Nevada in 1889 and taken up by the Arapaho, Cheyenne, Lakota, Caddo, Kiowa, Pawnee and other nations, whose followers danced in a circle with joined hands, singing without drums. Arapaho and Caddo Ghost Dance songs were recorded on cylinder in 1894 and their texts printed in The Ghost-Dance Religion (1896), and some songs are still sung among the Caddo of Oklahoma.",
